Context: this is the end of a chapter on finding the Moon's place from lunar eclipses, followed by the start of a chapter on the true node and inclination. In the earlier chapter Euler wrote the Moon's longitude in its orbit at mid-eclipse as the longitude opposite the Sun (the shadow's longitude) plus a reduction-type correction. That correction is multiplied by the coefficient (n+m+2k)/(n−m), where n, m and k are the hourly motions of the Moon, the Sun and the node. With mean values the coefficient is 1.17036, and this gave the small table on p.376.

§27 (end): the coefficient is insensitive to errors in the true hourly motions. With the worst case of n +300" and m −5" it drops by about 1/34. The largest tabulated equation (2'55" at 10° from the node) therefore changes by only about 5". That is negligible, because the observed middle of an eclipse cannot be fixed to such accuracy. Euler adds that the error could in any case be removed once true hourly motions are known.

§28: the other formulas of the chapter are not of this kind. In this coefficient n, which varies most, appears with the same degree in numerator and denominator, so its variation largely cancels. In the other formulas n does not, so they cannot be used with mean values. Without true hourly motions one cannot get:
- the instant when the Moon's orbital longitude equals the shadow's ecliptic longitude;
- the instant of opposition;
- the angle of the apparent lunar path with the ecliptic;
- still less the true node and inclination.
Hence the next chapter determines true hourly motions of Sun, Moon and node, and the apparent diameters of Moon and shadow (the latter depends on the lunar parallax).

New chapter, §1: before fixing the constants α, δ, ε of the lunar-longitude expression φ from observations outside syzygies, Euler first finds the true ascending node and inclination. Total eclipses are the most convenient source for this. He takes eclipse no. 6 (1722 June 28) and averages two observations:
- beginning 12h16'28", immersion 13h24'4", emersion 14h27'29", end 15h36'13";
- mid-times: (beginning+end)/2 = 13h56'20.5"; (immersion+emersion)/2 = 13h55'46.5";
- adopted middle 13h56'0" apparent time, i.e. 13h58'41" mean time (equation of time +2'41", plausible for late June).

From his own tables he computes the mean node, 3s2°20'43". The node equations are:
- −54" (Moon's mean anomaly);
- −12" (Sun's anomaly);
- +13'46" (argument node−Sun, the large Tychonic node inequality);
- 0 (Sun−Moon);
- +1'1" (node−Moon).
These sum to +13'41", so the true node is 3s2°34'24" = 92°34'24". The inclination is 5°16'59½" (argument node−Sun, the Tychonic variation of inclination) −42" +37" = 5°16'54". The following pages then compare these tabular values with values extracted from the eclipse observations themselves.
